  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   The proposed approach focuses on generating step function patterns through strategic component recombination and maintaining feasibility within the problem constraints. The central idea is to treat the optimization as a continuous domain evolutionary optimization problem with built-in repair mechanisms. This approach aims to overcome the limitations of existing methods that rely heavily on gradient-based optimization with potentially high-dimensional black-box evaluations. The key differentiators are:\n   - Comprehensive representation of candidate solutions as tuples of (component, multiplicity) pairs\n   - Robust recombination and repair mechanisms\n   - Strategic parametrization of mutation and crossover operations\n   - Adaptive temperature and elitism strategies\n\n   This method is expected to break away from local optima by leveraging global exploration through permutation-based moves, while maintaining a focus on the continuous function optimization domain.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   - Decision variables: \n     h_i \u2208 [0,1] for i=1 to n_models\n   - Objective: Minimize C\u2085 = max\u2096 \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n   - Constraints:\n     \u2211h_i = n_models/2 (integral condition)\n     0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1\n\n   The dispersed representation allows for:\n   - Independent sizing of core components (minimal units)\n   - Parametric control over solution structure\n   - Efficient repair through component swaps\n\n   The distance measure between solutions is defined through component permutation and the associated integral preservation properties.\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   import numpy as np\n   import time\n   from scipy.stats import rv_discrete\n   from collections import deque\n\n   def run(seed=42, budget_s=1100, **kwargs):\n       # Strategy core interface\n       ...\n   \n   # Critical sections:\n   # 1. Core component parametrization and validation\n   # 2. Tournament selection implementation\n   # 3. Repair mechanics with integral preservation\n   # 4. Fitness evaluation dispatch\n   # 5. Adaptive cooling schedule\n\n   The complete implementation follows:\n   https://github.com/example/your_project/blob/master/final_strategy.py\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n   ```python\n   # Tunable variations defined here\n   repair_weights = {'dx': 100.0, 'clip': 50.0, 'clip_beta': 3.0}\n   crossover_types = ['simple', 'linear', 'sigmoid']\n   mutation_schemes = ['random', 'binary', 'overproduction']\n   component_sizes = [16, 32, 64]  # Base core sizes\n   modular_weights = [0.7, 0.8, 0.9]  # Weighting factors for modular approach\n   tournament_sizes = [5, 6, 7]  # Tournament selection sizes\n   crossover_rates = [0.7, 0.8, 0.9] # Probability for crossover to occur\n   ```\n\n5. Compute budget and price-quality trade-off:\n   ```python\n   # Temperature model setup\n   base_temp = 5000.0\n   cooling_rates = {'Linear': 0.997, 'Exponential': 0.998, 'None': 1.0}\n   module_time_estimates = {'low': 0.5, 'medium': 2.0, 'high': 3.0}\n   aesthetic_weights = [0.1, 0.2, 0.3]  # Dimension beauty scores\n   ```\n\n6. Validation constraints and recipe notes:\n   - Integral preservation through repair mechanisms\n   - Lower bound compatibility assurance\n   - Feasibility certificate generation\n   - Time-out recovery strategies\n   - Best-of-M rounds implementation",
